diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f8357766959870f00767b398dea095570ca74005
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,187 @@
+# Low-rank infinity-norm approximation generator
+
+This is a **Track B** generator based on Gillis and Shitov, [*Low-Rank Matrix Approximation in the Infinity Norm*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.00078). It is a paper-licensed reduction family: the solver reasons about a NAE-3SAT encoding that defines the paper's sparse signed matrix, while the checker constructs and verifies rational rank-one factors. It should be counted as logic/reduction coverage, not as an unqualified native numerical-optimization family.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| native domain | `logic` |
+| object regime | `rational_exact` |
+| computational core | `csp_sat` |
+| certificate form | `exact_symbolic` binary string |
+| intuition | reduction recognition |
+| domain essentiality | `licensed_reduction` |
+| reduction | Section 3, Lemma 1 and Theorem 2 |
+
+## What the problem is
+
+The solver receives a shuffled NAE-3SAT formula and an exact sparse definition of the matrix \(M(G,D)\) used in Section 3. It must give the bits of designated base variables. The checker extends the degree-two auxiliaries locally, applies the paper's edge-switching construction, topologically sorts the switched graph, constructs explicit rational vectors \(u,v\), and checks \(\max_{ij}|M_{ij}-u_i v_j|\le k\) with `Fraction` arithmetic. The submitted bit string is therefore a finite executable witness, not a claim that the checker accepts by appealing to the theorem.
+
+Generation is inverse. It samples the base bit vector first, forms the cyclic system
+
+```text
+x[i] XOR x[i+1] XOR x[i+2] = b[i]       (indices modulo n),
+```
+
+compiles each parity equation to four 3-CNF clauses, each 3-CNF clause to two NAE clauses, and finally applies the paper's matrix reduction. Since `n` is never divisible by 3, the cyclic system is nonsingular over GF(2), so the planted base assignment is unique.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+The paper's Theorem 3 proves NP-completeness of rank-one infinity-norm approximation, using Theorem 2's signed-matrix reduction. Its hard construction has only the diagonal entries above the threshold, so the threshold graph has the maximum number of components—the opposite of Theorem 1's polynomial `d=O(log n)` regime. Lemma 1 and Corollary 1 also show why the initially suggested nonnegative/quantized rank-one planting is unsuitable: known signs reduce the task to polynomial linear feasibility, and nonnegative matrices are polynomial-time.
+
+This generated distribution is **not** claimed structurally hard. An efficient algorithm exists: pair the degree-two NAE auxiliaries, recover four forbidden assignments on every three-variable support, decode the GF(2) equations, and run Gaussian elimination. At shipping size, exact bit-packed RREF solved 8/8 instances in 0.00636 seconds mean time, using 305 mean row XORs = 14,664 mean scalar bit operations (maximum 18,336). The compact route recognizes that supports form one cycle and evaluates a second-order recurrence; its measured operation budget is 286 XORs. The benchmark is the gap between discovering that representation and mechanically eliminating the displayed encoding.
+
+## Worked `demo` example
+
+For `make_instance(n=5, seed=0, presentation_rounds=1)`, `render` returns:
+
+```text
+Certified rank-one approximation of a sparse signed matrix
+
+For a bit y in {0,1}, the positive literal +y has value y and the negative
+literal -y has value 1-y.  A triple is NAE (not-all-equal) when its three
+literal values contain at least one 0 and at least one 1.  Variable
+y0 is fixed to 0.
+
+There are 26 Boolean variables y0,...,y25.  Submit values only
+for the following 5 base variables, in exactly this displayed order:
+  y7, y10, y20, y22, y23
+Every other non-fixed variable is an auxiliary.  Each auxiliary occurs in one
+pair of clauses and may be chosen locally after the base bits are fixed.
+
+The 40 NAE clauses are:
+  c0: (+y0, -y17, -y10)
+  c1: (-y23, +y24, -y22)
+  c2: (+y0, -y1, +y22)
+  c3: (+y10, +y7, +y11)
+  c4: (+y13, -y7, +y20)
+  c5: (-y23, +y10, +y6)
+  c6: (-y10, +y0, -y24)
+  c7: (-y22, +y23, +y21)
+  c8: (+y18, -y23, -y10)
+  c9: (+y20, +y5, +y7)
+  c10: (-y8, +y23, +y0)
+  c11: (+y23, +y17, +y22)
+  c12: (-y10, +y16, +y23)
+  c13: (+y0, -y16, -y7)
+  c14: (+y23, +y10, +y4)
+  c15: (-y6, -y7, +y0)
+  c16: (+y0, -y25, -y23)
+  c17: (+y22, +y14, +y20)
+  c18: (-y22, +y0, -y5)
+  c19: (-y11, +y20, +y0)
+  c20: (-y7, +y12, -y10)
+  c21: (-y13, +y22, +y0)
+  c22: (+y0, -y14, -y23)
+  c23: (+y15, +y10, -y7)
+  c24: (+y0, -y12, +y20)
+  c25: (-y22, +y8, +y20)
+  c26: (+y19, -y20, +y22)
+  c27: (+y0, -y4, +y7)
+  c28: (-y9, -y20, +y0)
+  c29: (-y20, +y1, +y7)
+  c30: (-y20, -y22, +y25)
+  c31: (-y10, +y7, +y9)
+  c32: (-y19, +y23, +y0)
+  c33: (+y0, -y21, +y10)
+  c34: (-y22, +y0, -y2)
+  c35: (+y3, +y22, -y23)
+  c36: (-y20, +y0, -y15)
+  c37: (+y0, -y3, +y10)
+  c38: (+y2, -y7, -y20)
+  c39: (-y18, +y7, +y0)
+
+These clauses define an exact q-by-q integer matrix M, q=172, as follows.  This
+is a sparse inline definition; every entry not assigned below is 0.
+
+* For Boolean variable ya, define vertices P(a)=2a and N(a)=2a+1.
+* For clause ct and position h in {0,1,2}, define O(t,h)=2*26+3t+h.
+* M[i,i]=2 for every i from 0 through 171.
+* For every a, M[P(a),N(a)]=M[N(a),P(a)]=-1.
+* If position h of clause t is +ya, set both entries between O(t,h) and N(a)
+  to -1.  If it is -ya, set both entries between O(t,h) and P(a) to -1.
+* For each t, use directed edges O(t,0)->O(t,1), O(t,1)->O(t,2), and
+  O(t,2)->O(t,0).  For every directed edge r->s, set M[r,s]=-1 and M[s,r]=1.
+
+No pair receives two conflicting rules.  The threshold is the exact rational
+k=35500799/23667200.  Your base bits must extend to all auxiliaries so that every NAE
+clause holds.  The checker then applies the above matrix construction, reverses
+each directed edge whose endpoint bits differ, topologically orders the result,
+constructs explicit rational vectors u,v, and checks exactly that
+max(i,j) |M[i,j]-u[i]*v[j]| <= k.  Thus the submitted bits are a compressed,
+executable certificate for a rank-one approximation; no floating point is used.
+
+The answer is one string of exactly 5 characters from {0,1}.  Bit
+position 0 is the value of the first displayed base variable, and so on.
+Order matters and there are no separators.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as that binary string.
+Example format only: <answer>01010</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>11101</answer>`. `verify(inst, "11101")` returns `(True, "ok")`; dropping the last bit returns `(False, "bit string is too short: expected 5, got 4")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by decoding the five parity supports or by trying its 32 base strings.
+
+## Difficulty presets
