{"schema_version": 2, "model": "google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "effort": "medium", "params": {"n": 26, "decoys": 32}, "seed": 29384634, "solved": "solved", "parsed": true, "verify_ok": true, "verify_reason": "ok", "reply": "To find the values of the 26 base bits $y_0, \\ldots, y_{25}$, we can analyze the 58 dense parity equations given. \n\nEach equation consists of exactly 23 variables XORed together. Since there are 26 variables in total, exactly 3 variables are \"missing\" from each equation. Let $S = y_0 \\oplus y_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus y_{25}$ be the sum (XOR) of all 26 variables. \nAny equation can thus be rewritten as:\n$S \\oplus y_a \\oplus y_b \\oplus y_c = \\text{rhs}$\nwhich implies:\n$y_a \\oplus y_b \\oplus y_c = S \\oplus \\text{rhs}$\n\nLet's group equations by their missing variables to establish direct relationships between the bits:\n- **e0** (missing 5, 8, 12) has rhs=1 $\\implies y_5 \\oplus y_8 \\oplus y_{12} = S \\oplus 1$\n- **e30** (missing 0, 8, 12) has rhs=1 $\\implies y_0 \\oplus y_8 \\oplus y_{12} = S \\oplus 1$\nComparing these two gives **$y_5 = y_0$**.\n\n- **e8** (missing 6, 16, 21) has rhs=0 $\\implies y_6 \\oplus y_{16} \\oplus y_{21} = S$\n- **e21** (missing 2, 6, 16) has rhs=0 $\\implies y_2 \\oplus y_6 \\oplus y_{16} = S$\nComparing these two gives **$y_2 = y_{21}$**.\n\n- **e19** (missing 2, 16, 24) has rhs=0 $\\implies y_2 \\oplus y_{16} \\oplus y_{24} = S$\nComparing with e21 ($y_2 \\oplus y_6 \\oplus y_{16} = S$) gives **$y_6 = y_{24}$**.\n\n- **e36** (missing 2, 17, 24) has rhs=1 $\\implies y_2 \\oplus y_{17} \\oplus y_{24} = S \\oplus 1$\nComparing with e19 ($y_2 \\oplus y_{16} \\oplus y_{24} = S$) gives **$y_{17} = y_{16} \\oplus 1$**.\n\nBy systematically chaining these missing-variable XOR pairs, we can reduce all 26 variables to be expressed entirely in terms of $y_0$:\n- $y_0 = y_3 = y_5 = y_{10} = y_{11} = y_{15} = y_{17} = y_{18} = y_{19}$\n- $y_0 \\oplus 1 = y_1 = y_2 = y_4 = y_6 = y_7 = y_8 = y_9 = y_{12} = y_{13} = y_{14} = y_{16} = y_{20} = y_{21} = y_{22} = y_{23} = y_{24} = y_{25}$\n\nWe can determine $S$ and $y_0$ by checking the total XOR sum. We found 9 variables that evaluate to $y_0$ and 17 variables that evaluate to $y_0 \\oplus 1$.\nThus, $S = 9 \\cdot y_0 \\oplus 17 \\cdot (y_0 \\oplus 1) \\pmod 2$. \nSince 9 is odd and 17 is odd:\n$S = y_0 \\oplus (y_0 \\oplus 1) = 1$.\n\nWe also know from substituting into **e26** (missing 2, 9, 22 with rhs 0) that $y_2 \\oplus y_9 \\oplus y_{22} = S$. \nSubstituting our relations gives $(y_0 \\oplus 1) \\oplus (y_0 \\oplus 1) \\oplus (y_0 \\oplus 1) = S$, resolving to $y_0 \\oplus 1 = 1 \\implies \\mathbf{y_0 = 0}$.\n\nNow that $y_0 = 0$, we can resolve all the variables:\n- $y_0, y_3, y_5, y_{10}, y_{11}, y_{15}, y_{17}, y_{18}, y_{19}$ are **$0$**\n- $y_1, y_2, y_4, y_6, y_7, y_8, y_9, y_{12}, y_{13}, y_{14}, y_{16}, y_{20}, y_{21}, y_{22}, y_{23}, y_{24}, y_{25}$ are **$1$**\n\nAssembling this ordered from $y_0$ to $y_{25}$ generates our exact 26-bit binary string.\n\n<answer>01101011110011101000111111</answer>", "error": null, "elapsed_sec": 58.54, "http_status": 200, "finish_reason": "stop", "preset": "escalated", "escalation_round": 4}
